5,'I\'ve listened to western medicos like Dr. Dean Edell put down supplements and vitamins (and go ballistic over homeopathic remedies) for thirty years. Chinese meds are rarely mentioned, and when they are it\'s usually to say they\'re old and useless.<br /><br />Well, Chinese remedies are old: they have a couple thousand years on modern medicine.  And from experience, I think they have their place.  They certainly do for me.<br /><br />I first came upon Chinese remedies seven years ago.  My then six-year-old cat had chronic irritable bowel symptoms and my usual vet - open-minded as he was - could offer nothing but prednisone, a steroid.  Cats tolerate it much better than humans or dogs, but I knew it would shorten his life. That was when I found a DVM who also uses holistic methods.  Ricky the cat was put on a tea pill regimen (not the pill being reviewed) and has been on them ever since, with only an occasional change.  Ricky is extremely active and far healthier at thirteen than he was at six.<br /><br />So, I\'d been taking Inderol for mild high blood pressure for many years and I had avoided reading about the side effects for most of that time. A couple years into Ricky\'s Chinese pills, I consulted a practitioner and began using Six Flavor Tea Pills as recommended.  After a couple weeks to give the pills time to begin working, I began lowering the amount of Inderol I was taking.  Within a month, I was off the prescription drug and my blood pressure was lower than the drug-induced norm.  It\'s stayed that way since then.  (And one of the side effects of Inderol, tiredness, suddenly abated in the first couple months -- I was so used to the side effect that I didn\'t know I had it until it was gone.<br /><br />This is a remedy.  It may work for you, it may not.  Once my western MD commented on my better-than-ever blood pressure (at least six months after I\'d switched), I was pretty convinced this is good stuff. For me.  Please consult a good practitioner -- these simple pills are complicated and a practitioner prescribes or suggests OTC like this based on a whole body approach. Their goal is to restore balance to the body, and a good one knows what they\'re doing.<br /><br />Meanwhile, Ricky and I are very happy.<br /><br />Note that both practitioners (human and animal) only use Plum Flower Brand. Neither recommends any other brand.',B002U7FOAM,AEIW7GMVHQ4HBUXTTYEMHEMADUMQ
